<ul id="A2.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="A2.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="A2.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I3.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The learning rate for other benign passive parties is set to 1.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. </span><a href="#A2.F15" title="Fig. 15 ‣ Appendix B Additional Evaluation Results ‣ Is the Trigger Essential? A Feature-Based Triggerless Backdoor Attack in Vertical Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a><span id="A2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> illustrates the impact of Gaussian perturbation distribution on the performance of our attack when rASR is used as the metric. The experimental results align with those obtained using mASR as the metric.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F15" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F15.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20593v1/gaussian_mnist_1_auxiliary_rASR_True.png" id="A2.F15.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/197;" width="229" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>MNIST</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A2.F15.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20593v1/gaussian_cifar10_1_auxiliary_rASR_True.png" id="A2.F15.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:229/197;" width="229" height="197" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>CIFAR-10</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 15: </span>Impact of the Gaussian distribution on the ASR.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table </span><a href="#A2.T12" title="TABLE XII ‣ Appendix B Additional Evaluation Results ‣ Is the Trigger Essential? A Feature-Based Triggerless Backdoor Attack in Vertical Federated Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">XII</span></a><span id="A2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> summarizes the computational overhead in seconds for our proposed attack compared to traditional attacks across the test sets of five datasets. To ensure fairness and eliminate the influence of GPU and CPU arithmetic, all experiments are conducted on a four-core Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-7700 CPU. The results demonstrate that our attack requires computational resources comparable to those of other attacks, without introducing additional overhead.</span></p>
</div>
